    all 135716 digit multisets of length 4..40 over {1,3,7,9}: every one except the all-ones multiset has a composite permutation (2 s).  Hence the permutable primes with 4..40 digits are exactly R19 and R23.
    near-repdigits x..xy (x != y in {1,3,7,9}) of every length 4 <= n <= 20000: some permutation is divisible by a prime q <= 131 (and exceeds it) (29 s).
    Together with Johnson (1977; CITED via Wikipedia, not read: no permutable prime has three distinct digits, or two digits each used twice), this reproduces the no-non-repunit statement up to 20000 digits; Richert (1951; CITED via Wikipedia/De Geest, not read) states it for 3 < n < 6*10^175.

(3e) Why finitely many non-repunit circular primes are expected (heuristic, not a theorem)
    A k-digit circular prime (k >= 2) uses only the digits 1,3,7,9: a digit 0,2,4,5,6,8 rotated to the end
    makes that rotation divisible by 2 or 5.  Model: the k rotations are prime independently with
    probability 3.75/log N (coprime to 30), corrected at 3 (all rotations share the digit sum) and at every
    prime q | 10^k - 1 (rotation multiplies by 10 mod q, so the rotations are all or none divisible by q).
