(4d) Rotation <-> Collatz: rational cycles are shift orbits of parity words
    The shift S on Z_2 (drop the last binary digit) acts on the period-k points -B/(2^k-1) (B = the k-bit
    block) as rotation of the block: base-2 digit rotation.  T = Phi S Phi^-1, so the T-cycle of a primitive
    word w of length p is the image of the rotation orbit of w; its points are x_w = c_w/(2^p - 3^a).
    Fixed points of rotation (blocks 0^k, 1^k) <-> the two fixed points 0, -1 of T.  For prime p there are
    (2^p - 2)/p cycles of exact period p (Fermat, necklace form).  'All rotations prime' is not rotation-
    invariant; 'integral' is (T maps Z to Z along the cycle), so integral cycles are the Collatz analogue.
    integral periodic points with a primitive word of length <= 18 (3 s):
      period  1: word 0                    point 0
      period  1: word 1                    point -1
      period  2: word 10                   point 1
      period  3: word 110                  point -5
      period 11: word 11110111000          point -17
    = the five known integer cycles 0, -1, {1,2}, {-5,-7,-10}, {-17,...,-91} (one point per cycle), and no other integral cycle of period <= 18 (FINITE-EXACT; the known cycle-length bounds are far larger).
    Heuristic count of integral cycles of length p: sum_a C(p,a)/(p |2^p - 3^a|)  vs circular primes:
      p =  5: expected integral p-cycles 5.418e-01
      p = 10: expected integral p-cycles 1.544e-01
      p = 20: expected integral p-cycles 5.963e-02
      p = 30: expected integral p-cycles 5.334e-02
      p = 40: expected integral p-cycles 2.832e-02
      p = 50: expected integral p-cycles 2.087e-02
      p = 60: expected integral p-cycles 1.783e-02
    Both expectations decay (Collatz like 2^(-(1-h(log_3 2))p) = 2^(-0.05 p) up to Diophantine spikes at
    convergents of log_2 3; circular primes like (6.5/k)^k), so both 'finitely many' statements are
    heuristically natural; neither is proved (the Collatz one is a Diophantine problem at 2^p - 3^a).

(4e) The 3-adic side
    (3^k-1)/2 = 0 -> 1 -> 4 -> 13 -> 40 -> 121 -> ... under x -> 3x+1 (the unaccelerated odd step, the
    E-graph climb), and (3^k-1)/2 + 1/2 = 3^k/2: the base-3 repunits converge in Z_3 to -1/2, the fixed
    point of x -> 3x+1 (a 3-adic contraction).  Since T^(k+1)(2^k-1) = (3^k-1)/2, the k+1 steps of the
    longest rising run carry the k-th 2-adic approximant of the fixed point -1 (distance 2^k in Z_2)
    to the k-th 3-adic approximant of -1/2 (distance 3^k/2 in Z_3).  Every base-b repunit is the orbit
    of 0 under the affine map x -> bx + 1 with fixed point -1/(b-1): b = 4 is the sibling ladder
    S(p) = 4p+1 of the inverse tree (fixed point -1/3 = E(0)), b = 3 the E-graph climb, b = 2 the
    all-ones binary tail (fixed point -1 = T's hostile fixed point).

